claire (Docs): Morning — I'm opening a tiny refresh PR for the Quickstart hello-world: replace the legacy cURL samples with modern fetch, add a one-file local auth-proxy for ephemeral tokens, and a short WebSocket example for realtime. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/14001

ben (Eng): Thx. Note two runtime changes to reflect: param rename `max_output_tokens` -> `max_tokens`, and streaming endpoint unified at `/v1/responses/stream`. Also add header `x-rw-client-ver` in examples if possible.

maria (DevEx): For the auth-proxy, do we want a Node example or a tiny CLI one-liner using the `rw` tool? I can add both: a 10-line Node proxy and an `rw auth login --ephemeral` example. Also recommend env var `REDWOOD_EPHEMERAL_TOKEN`.

claire (Docs): Yes, include both. Keep the Node proxy minimal and explicitly warn: "do not commit tokens." Add a short callout: "Examples assume Hosted API unless noted."

ben (Eng): Small SDK note: TS method changed from `responses.create` to `responses.generate`. Update the snippets so copy/paste works.

ci-bot: preview ready: https://deploy-preview-14001--docs.redwood.ai.netlify.app 

maria (DevEx): Draft Node proxy to paste into README:

```js
// local-auth-proxy.js
const http = require('http');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
http.createServer(async (req,res)=>{
  const body = await new Promise(r=>{let d='';req.on('data',c=>d+=c);req.on('end',()=>r(d));});
  const up = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses',{
    method:'POST',
    headers:{'Content-Type':'application/json','Authorization':`Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_EPHEMERAL_TOKEN}`},
    body
  });
  res.writeHead(up.status,{'content-type':'application/json'});
  res.end(await up.text());
}).listen(3000);
```

claire (Docs): Nice and compact. I replaced cURL blocks with fetch and added the proxy. Also updated the Docker base in sample repo to `node:20-alpine`. Waiting on Ben to sanity-check runtime compatibility.

